6. ONE COMPUTER

6.1 A paid license is bound to one Computer. The Software derives a Device identifier from the Windows cryptography MachineGuid and checks a signed License file on that Computer. The check is local. A License file copied to another computer fails the check because the Device identifier will not match.

6.2 You will not bypass, patch, or share around that check. You will not publish a License file, a license-signing secret, a checkout session used to mint licenses for other people, or a method whose purpose is to unlock a Computer that was not paid for. Circumventing a technical measure that controls access to a paid feature can violate the Digital Millennium Copyright Act, 17 U.S.C. section 1201, and similar laws. This section does not forbid a reverse-engineering right that a statute gives you and that cannot be waived, and then only to the extent of that statute.

6.3 The paid license does not travel with a disk image, a copied folder, or a hardware or Windows change that changes MachineGuid, unless we agree in writing to reissue it. Reissue is discretionary, may require proof of the original purchase, and is never a right to run one purchase on two Computers at the same time.

6.4 You will keep the License file and any local secret to yourself. You are responsible for activity under that license, including activity by another person who uses your Windows session.

6.5 A virtual machine, a second boot, or a reinstall that produces a different MachineGuid is a different Computer and needs its own paid license if you want the paid features there.

6.6 The license is not a floating license, not a site license, and not a per-seat license. If several people use the Computer, you are responsible for all of them.


