14. PROMPTS, FILES, SCREEN, AND VOICE

14.1 You choose what Input leaves the Computer. When you send a prompt, attach a file, name a project folder, turn on screen context, or use voice, you instruct the Software to pass that Input to the Agent or local function you configured. You represent that you have every right, license, and consent required to do that, including from an employer, a client, a school, and any person whose face, voice, message, or document is included.

14.2 Do not submit material you lack the right to share. That includes another person's secrets, credentials, government identification numbers, payment card data, health information, and material a contract says you must not put into a tool of this kind.

14.3 Voice uses the microphone when you invoke it. You are responsible for notice to, and consent from, anyone else who would be recorded, and for wiretap, eavesdropping, and all-party-consent laws that apply where any participant is located. Those laws include, where they apply, statutes such as the California Invasion of Privacy Act, Florida's two-party consent statute, the Illinois eavesdropping law, and the Pennsylvania wiretap law. You will not secretly record a person where consent is required. You will not use voice to identify or verify a person.

14.4 If a law treats a voiceprint or similar identifier as biometric information, including the Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act, 740 ILCS 14, where it applies, you will not use the Software to collect a biometric identifier or biometric information of another person without the written consent and the disclosures that law requires. Delexo does not run Hooxie as a biometric identification system and does not keep a database of voiceprints.

14.5 Screen context can include whatever is visible on the display you attach, including other people's messages, faces, and documents. You will not capture a screen, or send a capture to an Agent, unless you are allowed to. You are responsible for a claim that a capture was an unlawful interception, an invasion of privacy, or a breach of confidence.

14.6 We do not use Input to train a Delexo model. The Agent you choose may use Input under its own terms. Read those terms before you send anything sensitive. Once Input is sent to an Agent, we cannot pull it back.

14.7 You will not use the Software to monitor an employee, student, household member, or other person unless you have a lawful basis and you have given any notice the law requires. Hooxie is not an employee-monitoring product, and we do not provide a monitoring policy for you to adopt.


15. WHAT THE AGENT DOES

15.1 An Agent may read files, propose or apply edits, run commands, install or remove packages, delete data, open network connections, and take other actions in the folder and the environment you expose. Those actions can be wrong, destructive, expensive, or insecure. They can spend a Cursor or API balance. They can commit secrets. They can break a build.

15.2 You are solely responsible for reviewing Output before you rely on it, merge it, deploy it, submit it, or let a command run. You will keep backups of work you cannot afford to lose. You will point the Agent only at folders, machines, and credentials you are willing to expose to an automated system that can be wrong.

15.3 Delexo does not watch the Agent, does not approve its edits, and does not promise a particular model, a particular style of code, or a correct result. We do not promise that code will compile, that it will be secure, that its dependencies are legally licensed, or that it meets a specification, a course rubric, or a client's statement of work.

15.4 You will not point the Agent at production systems, live customer data, or broad credentials unless you have authority and you accept the full risk of loss. You will not disable your own safeguards and then treat the result as our failure.

15.5 You acknowledge that automated coding tools are known to invent functions, misread a repository, and suggest code that resembles code the model was trained on. Similarity to someone else's code is your problem to check before you distribute Output.


16. CAPTURE EXCLUSION

16.1 When Detectable is off, the Software asks Windows to exclude the Hooxie window from standard screen capture. That request is not a security boundary, not encryption of the screen, and not a promise of invisibility. A person in the room, a camera pointed at the monitor, a second computer, a kernel capture driver, a remote-management tool, a non-standard graphics path, a Windows update, or a bug can show the window anyway. Failure can be obvious or silent.

16.2 You must not rely on Capture exclusion to keep the Software secret from anyone who is entitled to see your screen, or from anyone you have promised a complete view of the display.

16.3 You may use Capture exclusion only where you are allowed to keep a window out of a capture. You may not use it to deceive a person or an institution, including during a proctored exam, a monitored assessment, a hiring exercise, a workplace session in which undisclosed assistance or undisclosed software is forbidden, or any other process that requires you to be honest about help you are receiving.

16.4 Delexo does not encourage cheating, misrepresentation of authorship, or evasion of a monitoring control. This Agreement does not authorize those uses. If you use Capture exclusion in a setting where it is forbidden, you do so entirely at your own risk. That risk includes a cancelled score, a withdrawn offer, discipline, termination, loss of a credential, and a claim by a school, an employer, a test administrator, a proctoring vendor, or a platform.

16.5 Delexo is not a party to your school rules, employment contract, exam rules, or client agreement. We have no duty to configure Capture exclusion for a particular proctoring product, to certify that a product cannot see the window, to testify that you did not use the Software, or to take a side in a dispute between you and an institution.

16.6 You will turn Detectable on, or close the Software, whenever a rule that binds you requires the window to be visible or requires the Software not to be running.


