37. DISPUTES, INDIVIDUAL ARBITRATION, AND CLASS WAIVER

37.1 Talk to us first. Before starting arbitration or a court case, the complaining party will email a notice. Yours goes to support@delexo.store with the subject line "Dispute," the purchase email, a short description of the claim, and the relief you want. Ours goes to the purchase email. The parties will try in good faith to resolve the dispute for 30 days after the notice is received. Either party may still seek relief under section 32 during those 30 days. This notice requirement does not bar you from a consumer agency or a small-claims filing that a mandatory law lets you make without prior notice, but you agree to send the notice when the law allows us to ask for it.

37.2 If the dispute is not resolved in those 30 days, you and Delexo agree to binding individual arbitration, except that either party may bring an individual action in small-claims court if the claim stays in that court and qualifies. The dispute will not be decided by a judge or a jury except as this section allows.

37.3 The Federal Arbitration Act, 9 U.S.C. section 1 and following, governs this arbitration agreement, including its formation and enforcement. The arbitrator decides disputes about whether this section applies to a particular claim, except that a court decides any dispute about whether the class waiver is enforceable, and a court may grant the relief in section 32.

37.4 The arbitration will be administered by the American Arbitration Association under its Consumer Arbitration Rules if you are using the Software for personal or household use, and otherwise under its Commercial Arbitration Rules. The rules are published at https://www.adr.org. If the AAA cannot or will not administer the case, the parties will pick another established administrator, and if they cannot agree, a court may appoint one. The arbitration will be held by video, by telephone, or on written submissions. An in-person hearing, if the rules require one, will be in the county where you live or in another place you and we agree. The arbitrator may award any individual relief a court in that place could award on an individual claim, including individual injunctive relief needed to resolve your own claim. The arbitrator may not preside over a class, a consolidated set of claimants, or a representative action, and may not award relief for anyone who is not a named party.

37.5 CLASS WAIVER AND JURY WAIVER. TO THE MAXIMUM EXTENT PERMITTED BY LAW, YOU AND DELEXO EACH WAIVE THE RIGHT TO A TRIAL BY JURY AND THE RIGHT TO BRING OR PARTICIPATE IN A CLASS ACTION, A CLASS ARBITRATION, A COLLECTIVE ACTION, OR A REPRESENTATIVE ACTION, INCLUDING A PRIVATE ATTORNEY-GENERAL ACTION. CLAIMS MUST BE BROUGHT ON AN INDIVIDUAL BASIS ONLY. If a court refuses to enforce this waiver for a particular remedy, such as a public injunctive claim that the law says cannot be waived, that remedy alone may be sought in court, and the rest of the dispute stays in individual arbitration. If a court refuses to enforce the waiver as to the claim itself, the claim proceeds in court under section 38, and the other claims stay in arbitration.

37.6 You may opt out of section 37.2 through section 37.5 by emailing support@delexo.store within 30 days after you first accept this Agreement. The subject line must be "Arbitration opt-out." Include your full name and the purchase email. An opt-out does not change any other section, including the warranty disclaimer, the liability cap, and the indemnity. If you opt out, or if this arbitration agreement is found not to apply to you, section 38 is the forum for court claims. Opting out for one person does not opt out an organization, and opting out for an organization must be sent by an authorized representative.

37.7 If you are a consumer living in a country or a state that forbids pre-dispute arbitration agreements or class waivers with consumers, this section yields to that prohibition, and you may bring an individual claim in the courts of your place of residence.

37.8 If you are a consumer, the AAA consumer fee schedule applies, and we will pay the filing and arbitrator fees that those consumer rules require the business to pay. Each party otherwise bears its own attorney's fees and costs unless a statute or the rules require a different allocation. We will not seek our attorney's fees against a consumer unless the arbitrator finds that the claim was frivolous or brought for an improper purpose, and a statute allows the award.

37.9 The arbitrator will issue a reasoned written decision sufficient for a court to enter judgment. Judgment on the award may be entered in any court with jurisdiction. The arbitration and the filings are confidential except as needed to prepare the case, to obtain judgment, to complain to a regulator, or as the law requires. Confidentiality does not stop you from talking with a government agency.

37.10 Nothing in this section stops either party from reporting a matter to a government authority, or stops you from filing a charge or complaint with a consumer-protection agency. Those agencies can sometimes act for the public. This section does stop a private representative action to the extent section 37.5 is enforceable.


